WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:06.290
SIERRA: How does a war … end?

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:11.170
When the conflict in Ukraine first started,
a lot of people predicted that Russia would

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:12.170
win.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:13.170
But that hasn’t happened.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:18.600
In fact the Ukrainian army, backed by the
West, has succeeded in holding off Russian

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.760
forces and even reclaiming territory.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:27.260
But as Putin faces losses on the battlefield,
he appears to be growing more volatile.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.640
Things have not stopped escalating.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.290
And this got me thinking, how can a war like
this …end?

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:35.930
How does any war end?

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:39.400
I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It
Matters.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:45.739
Today, we turn once again to our very own
Richard Haass, to ask some big questions about

00:00:45.739 --> 00:00:50.770
war, compromise, and all the shades of gray
involved in negotiating peace.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:54.220
The following interview is largely uncut.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.160
Gabrielle SIERRA: All right.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.750
Richard Haass, welcome back.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.180
Richard HAASS: Always good to be with you,
Gabby.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:06.290
SIERRA: Season six of Why It Matters and you've
stopped by for your what's-up-in-the-world

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:07.330
update.

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:11.570
Unfortunately, we are still discussing the
war in Ukraine.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:16.500
I just sort of want to start by saying that
it seems like Putin's not doing as well as

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.850
he thought he would, we all thought he would,
which is a good thing in theory, but it seems

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:27.820
to be that it's becoming more and more volatile
as defeats pile up and it's kind of getting

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.840
scary even though he is losing.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.610
So just this morning, he launched a large-scale
missile attack that struck at least 10 cities

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:35.610
in Ukraine.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.270
So, is it good he's losing?

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:38.270
Is it bad?

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.270
Is he losing?

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.180
HAASS: There's 10 questions in there-

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.920
SIERRA: You're welcome.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.260
HAASS: Let me say a few things.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:50.369
The trends in the war on the battlefield are
against Russia, that is true.

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:52.229
Ukraine knows why it's fighting more.

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:56.509
Its troops are better trained, they're better
motivated, seem to be better armed, certainly

00:01:56.509 --> 00:01:58.280
better led.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.880
Every day that goes by, it looks like Ukraine
is regaining control of a bit of territory,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.369
but they've got a long, long, long ways to
go and it's always or normally, it is more

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:10.399
difficult to take territory than it is to
hold territory.

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:15.170
It's more demanding to be on the offensive
than the defensive.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.950
So even though the trends are going against
Russia on the battlefield, it could be a very

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.489
long playing out.

00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:23.120
There's nothing that's necessarily imminent.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:29.530
And just because we saw some pockets of collapse
in the Northeast, I don't believe it would

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:34.800
be right to generalize and say that the Russian
Army writ large is about to collapse.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.300
Point one.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.590
What I think we're seeing though from Mr.
Putin is what I would term or dub an indirect

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:41.590
strategy.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.569
He can't prevail on the battlefield against
Ukraine.

00:02:44.569 --> 00:02:50.110
So what he is trying to do is essentially
extend the battlefield, literally and figuratively.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:55.720
By that I mean with Ukraine, which had gotten
used to the idea that the war is something

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:59.590
that takes place on a front, but not in downtown
Kyiv.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:04.290
And indeed, when I was in Kyiv pretty recently,
a few weeks ago, it felt like any other European

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.290
capital.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:08.780
People were sitting around drinking coffee
in the cafes and going out for dinner and

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:10.700
strolling and so forth.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:17.140
These recent renewed missile attacks against
civilian targets are psychologically tough.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.519
Not just physically tough, given the casualties,
but psychologically tough.

00:03:21.519 --> 00:03:26.230
Plus they will likely make it more difficult
for Ukraine to economically rebuild.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:27.939
It adds a tremendous degree of risk.

00:03:27.939 --> 00:03:31.140
You can never be sure when there might not
be another attack.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:36.739
One thing that Putin is doing is taking the
war again to Ukrainian cities.

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:42.150
Secondly, he's taking the war to Europe and
his principal weapon is natural gas, and what

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:49.110
he's hoping is that in a long cold winter,
the Europeans will come to regret their support

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.780
economically and militarily for Ukraine.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:57.610
And then thirdly, by his nuclear threats,
he's hoping he can get the United States to

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:58.610
think twice.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:02.079
He wants to hear more and more voices in the
United States saying, "This game isn't worth

00:04:02.079 --> 00:04:03.209
the candle.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.500
Yes, we care about Ukraine.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:09.349
Yes, we care that territory is not acquired
by force."

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:15.030
But Putin is hoping that a lot of people say,
"Is this really worth a nuclear exchange?"

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.090
And what Putin is obviously hoping is Americans
decide not.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.300
So that's his strategy.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:20.550
We'll see how it plays out.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:25.310
I don't think, at the moment, he will succeed
in that, if I were a betting man, which of

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:26.310
course I’m not.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:30.680
But if I were a betting man, though I am sorry,
I did not bet on the Giants recently.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.630
But if I were a betting man, I would think
this war continues.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:38.750
SIERRA: I mean to bridge off of that, people
seem to be talking a lot more about nuclear

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:42.870
weapons, which honestly is kind of a first
for me in my life.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.940
How scared should I be?

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:51.150
And also, if a nuke is used there on the battlefield,
would it affect us?

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:56.449
HAASS: Look, Russia could use at least in
imagination or a principle, small nuclear

00:04:56.449 --> 00:04:59.070
weapons sometimes called tactical nuclear
weapons.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:04.280
They could be used on a battlefield or they
could be used in some demonstration area over

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:05.830
the ocean.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:09.880
It would be a way of backing or further reinforcing
these threats.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:13.330
If you use them over a battlefield, it could
have an immediate effect.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.910
There would be some radioactive particles
and all that.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:17.630
The wind could blow it in any number of directions.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:22.130
It wouldn't affect us, but it could affect
other parts of Ukraine, other parts of Europe,

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:23.319
other parts of Russia.

00:05:23.319 --> 00:05:28.960
Again, my own view is that Putin is more likely
to be threatening these than actually using

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:29.960
these.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:33.670
I understand the logic of the threat more
than I understand the logic of use, though

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:38.690
I could see the logic of a demonstration test,
not in a populated area.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:42.300
And one of the things we are doing, by we
I mean the United States government, to the

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:47.140
best of my knowledge, is communicating to
him with some degree of specificity why he

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:50.300
would regret any use of nuclear weapons.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.750
There's various ideas that have been bandied
about.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:58.040
I've talked about removing the constraint
on the use of American air power over Ukraine.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:01.970
Others have talked about, I think it was General
Petraeus, about taking out the Russian Black

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:02.970
Sea Fleet.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:07.670
But the idea would be, we have fought this
war indirectly through arming Ukraine and

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:12.440
if Russia crossed the threshold, we could
end that self-imposed restraint and we could

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.370
say, "The Russian forces in Ukraine would
be decimated."

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.740
Now, the danger there is obviously if we were
to do that, does that contribute to escalation?

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:20.740
SIERRA: Right.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:23.539
HAASS: I can't sit here and tell you there's
no chance it wouldn't.

00:06:23.539 --> 00:06:27.740
That is always though the case, and that was
the case in 1962 during the Cuban missile

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.000
crisis and so forth.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:35.080
And the only thing that is particularly worrisome
now is that this leadership of Russia, Vladimir

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:42.110
Putin, operates we think with fewer constraints
than his predecessors did, what 60 years ago,

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:43.610
which is quite a remarkable statement.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:49.410
But again, we've got multiple norms here and
we don't want the threat of nuclear use to

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:54.900
totally immobilize us because that will simply
encourage further threats of nuclear use by

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.169
Russia or others.

00:06:56.169 --> 00:07:01.840
We don't want to put sweeteners on the table
so Ukraine or anybody would say, "Wow, you

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:07.400
guys are only offering this up because you
want to avoid a nuclear exchange," that teaches

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.860
a terrible lesson, and if that were the case,
they would be right.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:15.720
So Putin has actually made it more difficult
for diplomats to do their work because there's

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.800
the danger now that it would look like a reward
for his threats, which is not something we

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.160
want to be doing.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:27.120
SIERRA: So I guess my bigger question is,
how do wars end?

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:28.550
Like I actually don't know.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:31.759
And from what you're saying, it's not very
simple.

00:07:31.759 --> 00:07:34.710
It's not like packing up and going home.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:35.710
How does this end?

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:37.979
HAASS: Look, wars can end in multiple ways.

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:41.259
One is that one side prevails over the other.

00:07:41.259 --> 00:07:45.720
Originally, it looked like Russia might do
it over Ukraine.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.050
That seems out of the question now.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:52.250
Could one imagine it going the other way where
Ukraine routes the Russian forces?

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.380
Maybe, but we're not there.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:59.050
The more likely scenario is neither side can
totally dominate the other.

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:04.819
The costs of continuing are judged to be high
by both sides, the human economic military

00:08:04.819 --> 00:08:05.819
cost.

00:08:05.819 --> 00:08:09.240
And that creates at least the possibility
of a negotiation.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:14.400
This is something I've done as a diplomat,
not as successfully as I would like.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.229
I've written about it as well.

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:21.650
But negotiations only succeed when you have
leaders who are willing and able to cut a

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:23.220
deal.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:24.770
Willing is a question of disposition.

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:27.350
Able is a question of political strength.

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:33.669
Now Mr. Putin clearly has the political strength
to cut a deal, though I expect he worries

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:38.620
that if he were to cut a deal that looked
overly generous towards Ukraine, he could

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:41.450
face a real challenge from the right.

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:45.890
I think he's more worried about a challenge
from the right than he is from anywhere else.

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.560
But because of that, I'm not sure he's willing.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.850
He also worries about any sign of weakness.

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:55.170
Indeed, the whole war was fought, I think,
less because of territory or Ukraine joining

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:57.110
NATO or anything like that.

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:01.110
I believe Vladimir Putin saw the example of
Ukraine.

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:07.710
This liberal, Slavic, tied to Europe country
on Russia's border, as an example that posed

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:12.720
an existential threat to Russia and therefore,
he wanted to pose an existential threat to

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:13.720
Ukraine.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:17.970
For Volodymyr Zelensky to compromise right
now would be hard.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:19.840
Ukrainians are not disposed to compromise.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:25.940
I came away from my time there and I was there
just when the military trends were shifting

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:28.140
in Ukraine's directions.

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:34.839
The combination of the war itself, the atrocities
at Bucha, Izium, and elsewhere and then the

00:09:34.839 --> 00:09:38.769
military progress, there weren't a lot of
doves left in Ukraine.

00:09:38.769 --> 00:09:44.120
Indeed, the prevailing view is not only that
Ukraine wants to recover all of its territory

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:49.961
that it had in 1991, but also that it wants
economic reparations to pay for the cost of

00:09:49.961 --> 00:09:52.740
the war and wants accountability for war crimes.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.460
So you have a situation where right now, I
think, there's precious little that's diplomatic

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:03.870
talk for no overlap between what Ukraine and
Russia could compromise on.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:06.180
Now, could that change at some point?

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:08.990
Yes, and I can think of all sorts of formulas.

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:14.300
Lots and lots of things but the bottom line
is at the moment, what usually prevents agreements

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:21.220
from happening from wars from formerly ending
is less the lack of imagination about a deal

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:25.870
than it is again the willingness and ability
of the respective leaderships to sign onto

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:26.870
one.

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:31.269
They have to believe they are better off with
a deal or worse off without a deal.

00:10:31.269 --> 00:10:33.660
And at the moment, I don't think that's the
case.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:39.769
I actually think both of them prefer an open-ended
war right now because that way, they don't

00:10:39.769 --> 00:10:42.740
have to put their name on the dotted line
of any compromise.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:43.740
SIERRA: Right.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:48.070
I mean, do you think that the US is going
to stay involved and interested in the war?

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:51.930
I mean, you don't look forward to something
that drags on for years and years, which we're

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:52.930
familiar with.

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:57.019
HAASS: Well, one of Vladimir Putin's hopes
is that the answer to your question is not,

00:10:57.019 --> 00:11:01.950
that either after the midterm elections here,
there could be a changed political landscape

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:03.890
in the United States.

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:07.880
Simply Americans have sometimes a collective
case of ADD.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:13.589
There might be other international situations,
more likely domestic situations, that absorb

00:11:13.589 --> 00:11:19.760
a lot of our energies, that, plus Europeans
might, during a cold winter, basically say,

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:24.190
"The costs of supporting Ukraine alienating
Russia aren't worth it."

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:29.460
Ukraine itself, Putin's hoping gets somewhat
demoralized by the renewal of shelling of

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:30.460
civilians.

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:35.510
So Putin is hoping that time does for him
what his army has not.

00:11:35.510 --> 00:11:38.920
SIERRA: Have there been situations like this
before where we had to provide someone with

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:45.649
an offramp or had to really balance between
giving someone some concessions while also

00:11:45.649 --> 00:11:48.600
not, I don't know, negotiating with terrorists
type thing?

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:52.330
HAASS: Let's take the historical parallels
in two ways.

00:11:52.330 --> 00:11:55.180
One is, could you have a somewhat open-ended
war?

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:58.330
Well, history is filled with wars that go
on a long time.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:02.700
And even now, the situation between North
Korea and South Korea, there's not formal

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:08.560
peace; there's an armistice, and it's more
the absence of war than anything remotely

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:10.089
looking like peace.

00:12:10.089 --> 00:12:16.420
In lots of situations, you have open-ended
conflicts that may flare up Israel and Palestinians,

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:17.810
Israel and Hamas.

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:24.470
And what happens is you have certain signaling,
ground rules almost about how you conduct

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:25.470
it.

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:28.650
And every once in a while, one or the other
sides breaks one of those understandings or

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:31.779
ground rules, pursue some option.

00:12:31.779 --> 00:12:36.370
There's a flare up, there may or may not be
some diplomatic contacts and then things get

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:37.960
tamped down again.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:44.260
A lot of the Middle East, parts of Asia, have
that kind of, neither peace nor war.

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:49.029
Right now, we clearly have war between Russia
and Ukraine, but we could have something that's

00:12:49.029 --> 00:12:50.070
not as intense.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:53.089
I think again, there's a lot of precedent
for that.

00:12:53.089 --> 00:12:58.420
The idea of offramps, which is an unfortunate
expression, but look, every negotiation, unless

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:03.140
it's unconditional surrender, and even those
have something in it for the other side.

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:07.340
So any agreement, by definition, usually has
concessions.

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:12.990
Either it's necessary to forge the agreement
or it's judged as desirable for what you want

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:15.520
to do afterwards, what your post-war goals
are.

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:19.880
Because very quickly, your goals post-war
might be different than your goals during

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:20.880
the war.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:24.089
That happened in Europe and Asia after World
War II because suddenly the Cold War became

00:13:24.089 --> 00:13:27.580
the context in which a lot of diplomacy was
carried out.

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:30.230
So, there's always things that are thrown
in.

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:34.000
I've been involved in negotiations in the
Middle East, India and Pakistan.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:37.920
I was the US envoy to the Cyprus crisis for
years.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:40.500
I was the US envoy in Northern Ireland.

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:43.649
And in all of those, you think of compromises.

00:13:43.649 --> 00:13:49.290
In any negotiation, people have to be willing
and able, as I said, to agree.

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:53.620
Well, able, the ability of people to sign
a deal sometimes depends upon, you're giving

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:58.820
them something they can go back to their own
constituency and say, "Hey, I didn't get everything

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:02.320
but I got this and I got this critical feature."

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:08.899
So you have to decide if you value or sincerely
want an agreement, it's preferable to you

00:14:08.899 --> 00:14:10.360
than not having one.

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:17.870
So the idea that you structure peace agreements
in that way in order to calm a situation and

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:24.120
provide the various parties with what they
need to enter into it, that's what the diplomacy's

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:25.120
all about,

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:26.120
SIERRA: Right.

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.850
But we're saying Russia, we're saying administrations,
but it's really Putin.

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:38.430
So how can I possibly feel safe in a modern
world where one dude can hold all this power

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:41.970
and decide whether or not he wants to say
yes to this?

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:43.230
It's one person.

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:46.389
HAASS: Well, there's many people in Ukraine
who would agree with you.

00:14:46.389 --> 00:14:50.690
When I had conversations there, there were
literally members of the government who said,

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:56.040
"Why in the world would I want to sign an
agreement with this guy?" Or to use your technical

00:14:56.040 --> 00:15:01.009
word, dude, as Jeff Bridges might put it,
given that it wouldn't be worth the paper

00:15:01.009 --> 00:15:02.930
it's written on.

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:06.410
There are clearly a lot of people in Ukraine
who think that, and my bet is, there's more

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:11.160
people who think it now than did a couple
of weeks ago because of atrocities in Izium

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:14.610
or the recent shelling of cities.

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:15.610
That's a legitimate concern.

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:20.980
When Henry Kissinger spoke here at the council
recently, he warned against over personalizing

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:25.570
this and he said, "Just deal with Russia,"
essentially.

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:26.570
SIERRA: Interesting.

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:30.440
HAASS: Because we can't control whether Vladimir
Putin or somebody else is there.

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:35.130
So if you're worried about cutting a deal
with Vladimir Putin, and you should be, then

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:41.389
the question is, how do you construct it in
such a way that you build incentives for his

00:15:41.389 --> 00:15:45.610
not breaking it and you build penalties in
if he were to.

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:50.010
I would think that entering into an agreement
with Vladimir Putin would have to take into

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:52.959
account that trust of him is, shall we say,
negligible.

00:15:52.959 --> 00:15:55.360
His track record speaks for itself.

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:59.069
HAASS: It's hard for me to imagine any agreement
with Vladimir Putin and Ukraine.

00:15:59.069 --> 00:16:04.110
But if one were to happen, it couldn't be
one, shall we say, that was faith-based, that

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:05.110
is out of the question.

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:08.259
SIERRA: And you think that this conflict could
keep going post Putin?

00:16:08.259 --> 00:16:12.360
HAASS: Well, that depends upon what would
a successor government look like.

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:17.500
Look, every time a war ends, particularly
when it ends poorly, you will have those on

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:21.519
the center left in Russia who say, "This was
a war that never should have been started."

00:16:21.519 --> 00:16:24.899
You will have those on the right who will
say, "The problem was not that Putin started

00:16:24.899 --> 00:16:27.579
the war, it's how he and the generals fought
it."

00:16:27.579 --> 00:16:32.150
And if you're of the latter school, if you
think the problem was how the war was prosecuted,

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:36.240
then the emphasis I think would be on improving
Russia's military.

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:38.490
You don't want to normalize with Ukraine.

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:44.500
With that kind of a successor leadership,
I don't see Russia becoming, needless to say,

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:46.519
reintegrated with Europe, at peace with Ukraine.

00:16:46.519 --> 00:16:52.270
Now, I can imagine whether it's immediately
after Putin or maybe one step after that,

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:57.170
things don't necessarily go in a linear way,
a leadership coming to power that does want

00:16:57.170 --> 00:17:01.760
to reintegrate Russia a bit with Europe, wants
to get out from under sanctions.

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:06.589
Indeed, I think it's a question of when, not
if, such a Russian leader comes out and one

00:17:06.589 --> 00:17:11.900
of the things we ought to do is say and do
things that make that more likely.

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:17.929
We ought to talk about our future vision of
Russia's relationship with the West and the

00:17:17.929 --> 00:17:18.929
United States.

00:17:18.929 --> 00:17:23.610
We ought to talk about a relationship between
Ukraine and Russia that we believe, at least,

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:26.939
takes into account certain Russian concerns.

00:17:26.939 --> 00:17:31.500
We ought to talk about our support for the
integrity of Russian territory.

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:35.610
We ought to respect Russia's right to choose
its own leadership.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:40.721
Again, what matters to us is not who leads
Russia, but how those individuals act, what

00:17:40.721 --> 00:17:42.870
kind of policies they pursue.

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:48.880
We want to deter Russian escalation, but we
also want to reassure Russia that our goals

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:54.049
are limited and our policies are conditioned
upon Russian behavior.

00:17:54.049 --> 00:17:59.670
We do not want a Putin or anyone like him
to basically say, "We have no choice or we

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:03.390
have nothing to lose by acting recklessly
because there's nothing in it for us if we

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:04.390
act differently."

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:09.410
We always want them to understand that there
is something in it for them if they were to

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:10.650
act responsibly.

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:15.730
SIERRA: Do you think that it's just really
hard for people to understand these types

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:20.150
of situations, to fully comprehend what has
to be given and taken?

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:24.850
HAASS: I once had a conversation with the
president that I was working for in the government

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:29.850
and I outlined something that was at least
as complicated as this and he gave me a withering

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:31.860
look.

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:33.660
I said, "I know, I know.

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:35.530
There's a lot of nuance there."

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:39.980
But I said, "I work for the Department of
Nuance," since I worked for the Department

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:41.299
of State at the time.

00:18:41.299 --> 00:18:43.500
He did not find that a winning argument.

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:45.700
But it's complicated.

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:48.570
It's going to be complicated to design.

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:53.309
Even more, it's going to be complicated to
sell, to sell it domestically here, to sell

00:18:53.309 --> 00:18:57.760
it to the Russians, to sell it to Europe,
to sell it to Ukraine.

00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:01.659
This is going to be extraordinarily difficult
to orchestrate.

00:19:01.659 --> 00:19:05.580
And it may not be possible, but it's certainly
worth trying.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.440
Look, diplomacy is an instrument of national
security.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:09.470
We've got to do it.

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:11.990
I think we've got to think, consult.

00:19:11.990 --> 00:19:17.460
Ultimately, we'd have to explain it and not
everyone's going to agree.

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:19.200
Okay. that's always the way it is.

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:23.360
But I take your point, and there might be
a lot of grays and people may not be in the

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:24.360
mood for grays.

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:27.020
They might want a lot of black and whites.

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:31.590
There'll be a lot of people, particularly
in Ukraine, understandably a hundred percent,

00:19:31.590 --> 00:19:37.070
who have zero disposition to forgive and forget,
to compromise, to let people off the hook

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:43.740
on war crimes, on reparations, on sanctions,
on this on that, on accepting any limits on

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:46.850
what Ukraine can do on its territory.

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:48.809
That's something we're going to have to take
into account.

00:19:48.809 --> 00:19:52.230
Could there be some differences of opinion
between the United States and Ukraine?

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:53.230
Absolutely.

00:19:53.230 --> 00:19:54.230
That could happen.

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:57.360
We've had differences of opinion between the
United States and close allies before, we

00:19:57.360 --> 00:19:58.870
have it all the time.

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:02.130
Being a close ally doesn't mean you have identity
of views.

00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:06.480
It means you have a commitment to a larger
relationship and to larger purposes despite

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:08.590
the absence of identity of views.

00:20:08.590 --> 00:20:11.300
We could have that with Ukraine, we could
have that with Europe.

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:12.300
That's okay.

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:14.720
That's why God invented diplomats.

00:20:14.720 --> 00:20:17.970
SIERRA: Not nearly as satisfying though as
a...

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:20.280
HAASS: No, but Russia's a sovereign country.

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:25.000
It doesn't have a terribly capable conventional
military, but it does have a meaningful nuclear

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:28.490
arsenal, has significant energy resources.

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:31.160
It's an important country in the center of
Europe and Asia.

00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:37.850
So it is in our long-term interest that Russia
is reintegrated in the world on terms that

00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:41.020
we can mutually live with.

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:45.600
And that's going to be a very difficult conversation
with ourselves, with Ukraine, with others

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:51.940
because Russia under Putin has caused and
will continue to cause enormous damage to

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:53.960
people, to property, to principles.

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:56.860
But we may need to have that conversation
one day.

00:20:56.860 --> 00:21:02.130
We're not there yet, but indeed, we're very
likely to have to have that conversation one

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:03.130
day.

00:21:03.130 --> 00:21:06.160
It'll be difficult in the consultations in
private, it'll be extraordinarily difficult

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:12.340
in public in terms of explaining what will
look to some people as a compromise or even

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:14.420
an abdication of principle.

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:20.810
All you can do in those situations, I believe,
is get out there and explain why you believe,

00:21:20.810 --> 00:21:27.000
however flawed or imperfect this proposal
is, it is less imperfect than the alternatives

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:30.840
that would come to pass if you didn't go down
that path.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:31.840
That is the best you can do.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:32.870
It's not satisfying.

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:34.280
SIERRA: It is not satisfying.

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:35.409
HAASS: I hear you.

00:21:35.409 --> 00:21:36.409
I hear you.

00:21:36.409 --> 00:21:40.799
That said, I look forward to the day we are
there because that would be considerably preferable

00:21:40.799 --> 00:21:43.669
to where we are and are likely to be for some
time.

00:21:43.669 --> 00:21:49.059
SIERRA: Well, I have one sort of last big
picture question for you and a comment which

00:21:49.059 --> 00:21:53.820
is, talking about the world makes my friends
very unhappy.

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:58.350
They feel very bleak about the outlook of
the world.

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:00.120
HAASS: Is this where I tell you, you need
new friends?

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:01.140
SIERRA: Yes, exactly.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:06.779
So short of getting new friends, I mean, are
we always going to live in a world where there's

00:22:06.779 --> 00:22:07.779
a war, there's a war going on?

00:22:07.779 --> 00:22:09.700
Is this just the way that it's going to be
forever?

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:12.320
HAASS: So much I could say, a couple things.

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:18.100
One is, if you look at the last 75 years since
World War II, one of the remarkable things

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:22.060
of this era is there was not a war between
the major powers of the day.

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:26.390
Yes, there were wars in Vietnam, Iraq, I understand,
but there was not a war between the United

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:30.350
States and the Soviet Union or the United
States and China.

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:37.460
After the Chinese Civil War, France and Germany
and Britain integrated in the European Union.

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:41.980
I mean, look at the contrast between the first
half of the 20th century on the second half

00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:43.850
of the 20th century, night and day.

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:49.190
Also, during the last 70, 75 years, you can
tell your friends, that there was remarkable

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:50.190
progress.

00:22:50.190 --> 00:22:52.460
A lot of the world became democratic.

00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:56.930
The colonial period came to an end, not always
peacefully, but it came to an end.

00:22:56.930 --> 00:23:00.660
The average lifespan increased by decades
around the world.

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:05.620
Tremendous scientific progress in medicine,
health across the...

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:08.730
Living standards multiplied by orders of magnitude.

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:10.780
So there was enormous progress.

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:15.690
So I don't believe that history is simply
bad or negative.

00:23:15.690 --> 00:23:21.080
In some ways, it goes back to my favorite
book in the field, this book by an Australian

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:24.110
called Hedley Bull, The Anarchical Society.

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:28.430
At any moment in history, you have forces
of anarchy the things that tear us apart,

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:34.070
centrifugal forces, conflict, and you have
forces of society, things that bring us together.

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:37.179
One of the phrase we always hear is international
community.

00:23:37.179 --> 00:23:40.909
Well, things that do make a little bit more
of a society or community around the world

00:23:40.909 --> 00:23:47.100
where we team up to deal with aggression or
we team up to deal with disease or to regulate

00:23:47.100 --> 00:23:52.340
the internet or whatever the particular causes,
or to stop the spread of nuclear weapons.

00:23:52.340 --> 00:23:55.970
At any moment in history, there's always a
balance between these two.

00:23:55.970 --> 00:24:00.720
To me, one way to think about foreign policy
is how do you shift the balance away from

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:04.200
anarchy somewhat in the direction of more
society.

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:05.640
It's really turning a dial.

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:06.840
It's not a switch.

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:08.950
You're never going to get all good stuff.

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:10.809
God willing, you'll never get all bad stuff.

00:24:10.809 --> 00:24:14.770
What you want to do is change the balances,
you want to shift things slightly in a good

00:24:14.770 --> 00:24:15.770
direction.

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:19.070
And I think for a big chunk of the last 75
years, we did just that.

00:24:19.070 --> 00:24:22.700
I don't think we've done just that over the
last 10 or 15 years.

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:25.620
I think the dial's moving in the wrong direction.

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:30.920
And suddenly here, this conversation we've
had, it's actually one of those hinge moments.

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:36.370
And I believe a lot of lessons are going to
be learned about whether aggression pays,

00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:38.690
whether might makes right.

00:24:38.690 --> 00:24:44.409
Whether the West is a concept that actually
has meaning, whether alliances still work

00:24:44.409 --> 00:24:45.720
and so forth.

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:51.010
This is one of those moments, tell your friends,
that if we get this right, it could yield

00:24:51.010 --> 00:24:52.340
significant benefits.

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:57.500
It's not peace for the rest of time, but it
could move things in a better direction.

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:00.710
If we get this wrong, bad situations could
become worse.

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:05.390
What Russia would do elsewhere in Ukraine
or Europe, what the Chinese might do, based

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:09.200
upon this, against Taiwan, what North Korea
might do, what an Iran might do.

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:12.560
So this is one of those moments where there's
a lot of lessons being learned.

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:15.980
What we want to make sure is that the right
lessons are learned.

00:25:15.980 --> 00:25:21.110
And if that's the case, then I believe as
awful as this has been, this act of aggression,

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:22.490
some good will come of it.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:24.110
SIERRA: You heard it here, folks.

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:26.020
Richard Haass, optimist.

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:27.650
It's happening.

00:25:27.650 --> 00:25:29.250
This has been great.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:30.250
Thanks for chatting.

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:34.990
HAASS: Thanks for having me back.

00:25:34.990 --> 00:25:38.170
SIERRA: Of course, anytime.

